    "id": "how-to-stop-buyers-forwarding-your-notion-template-link",
    "uuid": "c9e2f4a1-6b3d-4e8f-9a7c-2d5b8e1f4a63",
    "slug": "how-to-stop-buyers-forwarding-your-notion-template-link",
    "title": "How to Stop Buyers Forwarding Your Notion Template Link",
    "html": "<article><p>You sell a Notion template for $29. A week later, someone posts the link in a Discord server with 4,000 members. Every one of them can now open your template and click \"Duplicate.\" You'll never see a sale from any of them.</p><p>This is the most common complaint among Notion template sellers, and the honest answer is that you can't make sharing impossible. Notion has no DRM. Once a buyer duplicates your template into their own workspace, that copy is theirs to keep.</p><p>What you <em>can</em> do is make forwarding much less useful and much easier to spot. Here's how.</p><h2 id=\"why-forwarding-is-so-easy\">Why forwarding is so easy</h2><p>Most template sellers deliver through Gumroad, Payhip, or Lemon Squeezy. Payment happens there, and the delivery email contains a Notion \"Share to web\" link.</p><p>That link is the whole product. Notion's own help guide describes \"Allow duplicate as template\" as letting anyone copy your page into their own workspace. There's no login, no password, and no limit on how many people use it. Forwarding the link forwards the product.</p><p>There are really three ways your template leaks:</p><ol><li><strong>The raw link gets forwarded.</strong> A buyer pastes your notion.site URL into a group chat, a subreddit, or a \"free templates\" list.</li><li><strong>The link and its password get forwarded together.</strong> If you add a password, some buyers will share both.</li><li><strong>A duplicated copy gets republished.</strong> Someone duplicates your template, turns on \"Share to web\" in their own workspace, and passes around their copy.</li></ol><p>Each one needs a different fix.</p><h2 id=\"step-1-lock-down-notion-settings\">Step 1: Tighten your Notion share settings</h2><p>Start with what Notion gives you for free:</p><ul><li><strong>Turn off search engine indexing.</strong> In the \"Share to web\" panel, make sure indexing is off. Otherwise Google can index your template page and send strangers straight to it.</li><li><strong>Use \"Link expires\" if you're on a paid Notion plan.</strong> Notion added auto-expiring public pages in October 2022. You can set public sharing to stop after an hour, a day, a week, or on a custom date. This works well for a launch window or a cohort, but it applies to everyone at once, so it's not a fit for an evergreen product.</li><li><strong>Keep your master template separate.</strong> Share a dedicated \"buyer copy\" page, not the page you edit. If that link leaks badly, you can turn off sharing on it and publish a fresh copy without touching your working version.</li></ul><h2 id=\"step-2-put-a-gate-in-front-of-the-link\">Step 2: Put a gate in front of the link</h2><p>A public Notion link has no password option. <a href=\"https://www.notions11.com\">Notions11</a> adds one. You paste your Notion share link, set a password, and get a <code>notions11.com/your-template</code> URL. Anyone who opens it has to enter the password before they're forwarded to the Notion page.</p><p>In your Gumroad or Payhip delivery email, send the Notions11 link and the password. Never send the raw notion.site URL.</p><p>This stops the most common leak: the bare link dropped into a list of free templates. Someone who finds only the URL hits a password screen and goes no further. For a full walkthrough, see <a href=\"https://www.notions11.com/blog/how-to-password-protect-a-notion-page\">How to Password Protect a Notion Page</a>.</p><h2 id=\"step-3-rotate-the-password\">Step 3: Rotate the password when it leaks</h2><p>Passwords get shared too. The fix is to change them. In Notions11 you can edit a link's password at any time without changing the URL.</p><p>A simple routine:</p><ul><li>Change the password on a schedule, such as monthly.</li><li>Update the password in your Gumroad or Payhip delivery message so new buyers get the current one.</li><li>For existing buyers, add a line to your product page: \"Lost access? Reply to your receipt and I'll send the current password.\"</li></ul><p>Buyers who already duplicated the template keep their copy, so rotating doesn't hurt them. It only cuts off people who were given an old password secondhand.</p><h2 id=\"step-4-limit-and-watch-access\">Step 4: Limit and watch access</h2><p>With <a href=\"https://www.notions11.com/prosignup\">Notions11 Pro</a> ($5/month), you can set a link to stop working after a set number of opens or on a specific date. You can also log the IP address and location of each visit and get an email every time the link is opened.</p><p>That data is how you notice a leak. A typical buyer opens a template link once or twice, duplicates it, and never returns. If a link sold to 40 buyers suddenly shows 300 opens from 25 countries in one afternoon, it's been posted somewhere.</p><p>For bigger sales, such as a $150 business OS or a team license, you can go further and create one Notions11 link per buyer with an access limit of a few opens. There's no cap on how many links you create. If that buyer's link starts getting traffic from all over the world, you know exactly whose copy leaked. More on reading this data in <a href=\"https://www.notions11.com/blog/how-to-track-who-opened-your-notion-link\">How to Track Who Opened Your Notion Link</a>.</p><h2 id=\"step-5-handle-republished-copies\">Step 5: Deal with republished copies</h2><p>A gate protects your link. It can't protect a copy someone has already duplicated and republished from their own workspace. For that, you have two tools:</p><ul><li><strong>Clear license terms.</strong> Put a short \"personal use only, no resale or redistribution\" note on your product page and at the top of the template itself. It won't stop a determined pirate, but it makes your position clear.</li><li><strong>Notion's report flow.</strong> Notion's help center says anyone viewing a public Notion page can open the ••• menu, choose \"Report page,\" and select a DMCA takedown request. That leads to Notion's DMCA policy page with instructions for filing a formal notice.</li></ul><p>Save takedowns for real redistribution, like a full copy of your template posted in a public \"free templates\" database. Chasing every single forward will take more time than the lost sales are worth.</p><h2 id=\"one-limit-to-know\">One limit you should know about</h2><p>Notions11 gates the Notions11 link, not the Notion page itself. After someone enters the password, they're forwarded to your original notion.site URL, and that URL stays public. A buyer who copies it from their address bar can share it directly.</p><p>That's why the steps above work together. The gate stops casual forwarding. Rotating passwords and keeping a separate buyer copy let you recover when a link spreads. If the raw Notion URL starts circulating, you can turn off sharing on that buyer copy, publish a new one, and point your Notions11 link at it. Buyers keep the same Notions11 URL, and the leaked Notion URL stops working.</p><h2 id=\"quick-checklist\">Quick checklist</h2><ul><li>Search engine indexing off on your template page</li><li>A dedicated buyer copy, separate from your master</li><li>Delivery email contains a Notions11 link and password, never the raw Notion URL</li><li>Password rotated on a schedule</li><li>Access logs checked for spikes (Pro)</li><li>License terms on the product page and inside the template</li></ul><p>You won't stop every forward. You can make sure a forwarded link usually goes nowhere, and that you find out when it doesn't. <a href=\"https://www.notions11.com\">Create your first protected Notion link</a> for free.</p></article>",
