    "id": "how-to-track-who-opened-your-notion-link",
    "uuid": "7f8a9b0c-1d2e-4f3a-b4c5-d6e7f8a9b0c1",
    "slug": "how-to-track-who-opened-your-notion-link",
    "title": "How to Track Who Opened Your Notion Link",
    "html": "<article><p>You send a Notion link — a proposal, course materials, a template, a client brief. Then you wait. Did they open it? When? Did the right person open it, or did someone forward the link?</p><p>Notion has no built-in way to answer any of those questions. When you share a page as \"Share to web,\" you get no data on who viewed it or when. There's no read receipt, no viewer log, no notification.</p><p>Here's how to actually know when someone opened your Notion link.</p><h2 id=\"what-notion-tells-you\">What Notion tells you (and doesn't)</h2><p>Notion's analytics are workspace-level, not link-level. In paid Notion plans, you can see page views and edits within your team workspace. But that data is about your own team's activity — not external viewers who open a public share link.</p><p>When you share a public Notion link with someone outside your workspace, Notion tracks nothing about that viewer. No timestamps. No IP addresses. No view count.</p><h2 id=\"tracking-with-notions11\">Tracking with Notions11</h2><p><a href=\"https://www.notions11.com\">Notions11</a> adds tracking to any public Notion link. When you create a Notions11 link wrapping your Notion page, each time someone opens the link, Notions11 records it.</p><p>With the free tier, you get basic view count — how many times the link was opened.</p><p>With <a href=\"https://www.notions11.com/prosignup\">Notions11 Pro</a> ($5/month), you get:</p><ul><li><strong>Email notification when accessed</strong> — you get an email each time someone opens your Notions11 link, with a timestamp</li><li><strong>IP address per access</strong> — the IP of the device that opened the link</li><li><strong>Location data</strong> — city and country per access</li><li><strong>Access log in your dashboard</strong> — full history of every open, with timestamps and locations</li></ul><h2 id=\"setting-it-up\">Setting it up</h2><ol><li>In Notion, copy your \"Share to web\" link.</li><li>Paste it at <a href=\"https://www.notions11.com\">notions11.com</a>.</li><li>In the link settings, enable \"Track recipient locations\" and \"Email me when accessed.\"</li><li>Save. Share the Notions11 link instead of the original Notion URL.</li></ol><p>From that point, every time someone opens the link, you'll see it in your dashboard and receive an email. The original Notion page is unchanged — Notions11 sits in front of it as a gate and tracker.</p><h2 id=\"who-this-is-useful-for\">Who this is useful for</h2><p><strong>Freelancers and agencies:</strong> You send a client a Notion proposal or project brief. You want to know if they've actually opened it before you follow up. With Notions11, you get an email the moment they do — with their approximate location, so you can tell if it was them or someone else on their team.</p><p><strong>Template sellers:</strong> You're selling a Notion template. After purchase, buyers receive a Notions11 link with a password. You can monitor how many buyers actually access the template, flag unusually high access counts (someone sharing the link widely), and see which countries your buyers are in.</p><p><strong>Educators:</strong> You share course materials with enrolled students as a Notions11 link. The access log shows you which students have opened the materials and which haven't — without requiring students to log in to anything or have a Notion account.</p><p><strong>Anyone sending a proposal:</strong> \"Did they see my proposal?\" is one of the most common questions in consulting and sales. Notions11 answers it with a timestamped email.</p><h2 id=\"what-tracking-shows\">What tracking shows — and what it doesn't</h2><p>Notions11 logs each time someone successfully passes through the gate (enters the correct password, if one is set) and gets forwarded to your Notion page. It logs the IP, the timestamp, and the location derived from that IP.</p><p>It doesn't tell you how long they spent on the Notion page, which sections they read, or whether they duplicated the template. That level of tracking would require instrumentation on the Notion side, which isn't possible.</p><p>For most use cases — \"did they open it?\" and \"who opened it?\" — the tracking is sufficient.</p><h2 id=\"comparison\">Comparison: Notion vs Notions11 for link tracking</h2><table><thead><tr><th>What you want to know</th><th>Native Notion</th><th>Notions11</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Did someone open my shared link?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>When did they open it?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>How many times was it opened?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>What country did the access come from?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (Pro)</td></tr><tr><td>Get an email when it's opened</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (Pro)</td></tr><tr><td>See the full access history</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (Pro)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Related: <a href=\"https://www.notions11.com/blog/how-to-password-protect-a-notion-page\">How to password protect a Notion page</a> · <a href=\"https://www.notions11.com/blog/how-to-set-an-expiry-date-on-a-notion-link\">How to set an expiry date on a Notion link</a></p></article>",
