    "slug": "notions11-vs-super-so",
    "title": "Notions11 vs Super.so: Which Should You Use to Gate Notion Content?",
    "html": "<article><p>If you want to control who can access your Notion content, two tools come up most often: Notions11 and Super.so. They both put some form of gate in front of Notion pages — but they're built for different things.</p><p>Here's an honest side-by-side, so you can pick the one that matches what you actually need.</p><h2 id=\"what-each-tool-does\">What each tool does</h2><p><strong>Notions11</strong> is a link-gating service. You paste a Notion share link, and Notions11 generates a password-protected URL at <code>notions11.com/your-slug</code>. Your Notion page stays exactly as it is. The gate sits in front of it. That's it.</p><p><strong>Super.so</strong> is a Notion site builder. It takes your Notion workspace and publishes it as a full website on your own domain — with custom fonts, custom CSS, SEO settings, and optional password protection per page. The password feature is one small part of a larger platform.</p><p>The key difference: Notions11 adds a gate to a Notion link. Super.so rebuilds your Notion page as a standalone website that can have a gate.</p><h2 id=\"feature-comparison\">Feature comparison</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Notions11</th><th>Super.so</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Password protection</td><td>Yes (all plans)</td><td>Yes (paid plans)</td></tr><tr><td>Custom domain</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>No-code site builder</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Link expiry (by date)</td><td>Yes (Pro)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>View-count limit</td><td>Yes (Pro)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Access notifications (email)</td><td>Yes (Pro)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>IP and location tracking</td><td>Yes (Pro)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Setup time</td><td>Under 1 minute</td><td>Several hours</td></tr><tr><td>Price</td><td>Free / $5/month Pro</td><td>~$16/month Starter</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id=\"when-notions11\">When Notions11 is the right choice</h2><p>Notions11 makes sense when:</p><ul><li><strong>You want a password gate quickly.</strong> Paste a link, set a password, share. Under a minute from start to finish.</li><li><strong>You're selling or distributing Notion templates.</strong> The password goes in the buyer's delivery email. Anyone who forwards just the link hits a gate.</li><li><strong>You want to know when someone accessed your content.</strong> Notions11 Pro emails you each time someone opens your link, and logs the IP and location.</li><li><strong>You need access to expire.</strong> By date (&ldquo;expires November 30&rdquo;) or by view count (&ldquo;first 100 opens only&rdquo;). Super.so doesn't have either.</li><li><strong>You don't need a custom domain.</strong> Your link will be at <code>notions11.com/slug</code>, not your own domain.</li></ul><h2 id=\"when-super-so\">When Super.so is the right choice</h2><p>Super.so makes sense when:</p><ul><li><strong>You want a proper website with your domain.</strong> <code>yourbrand.com</code> instead of <code>notions11.com/slug</code>.</li><li><strong>You care about design control.</strong> Super.so lets you override Notion's default styling with custom CSS, fonts, and layouts.</li><li><strong>You're building a public site that also has some private sections.</strong> A public blog with a password-protected members area, for example.</li><li><strong>You're publishing a course platform or portfolio.</strong> Super.so is closer to a full website builder; Notions11 is a link tool.</li></ul><h2 id=\"what-about-potion\">What about Potion?</h2><p>Potion is another Notion site builder in the same category as Super.so — custom domain, site design, optional password. Similar price range, similar use cases. If you're comparing site builders for Notion, evaluate both Potion and Super.so. If you just want a gate on a link, neither is the right tool.</p><h2 id=\"the-honest-take\">The honest take</h2><p>These tools don't really compete — they solve different problems. Super.so is for people who want a website built on top of Notion. Notions11 is for people who want to add a password or access control to an existing Notion link without rebuilding anything.</p><p>If you're a Notion template seller who wants buyers to get gated access to your template after a Gumroad purchase: Notions11. If you want your entire Notion-built portfolio or course site to live at your own domain with a polished design: Super.so.</p><p>Both are legitimate choices. Most people who end up at the Notions11/Super.so decision actually want Notions11 — they want a gate, not a full site rebuild. If that's you, <a href=\"https://www.notions11.com\">start here</a>.</p><p>Related: <a href=\"https://www.notions11.com/blog/how-to-password-protect-a-notion-page\">How to password protect a Notion page</a> · <a href=\"https://www.notions11.com/blog/how-to-share-a-notion-template-privately\">How to share a Notion template privately</a></p></article>",
